<Window x:Class="HolyLogger.TryAgainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="Try Again" MinWidth="380"
        SizeToContent="WidthAndHeight" MaxHeight="720" MaxWidth="900"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterScreen" ResizeMode="CanResize"
        Icon="Images/crown.png"
        Background="#E8F5E9"
        Foreground="#1A1A1A">
    <!-- THE STATIONS THE OPERATOR MEANT TO COME BACK TO.
         Filled from the cluster's right-click menu, emptied by working them. The list lives in the
         database, not in this window, so closing the program does not lose it - the whole point of
         "try again" is that the try does not have to be today.

         AS TALL AS THE LIST IS LONG (SizeToContent="Height"). Two rows get a two-row window; there is
         no empty green field under the last station. The width and the position are still the
         operator's own and are remembered - the HEIGHT is not his to keep, because it is worked out
         from the list every time it is shown.

         AND AS WIDE AS THE TABLE IS WIDE - no Width at all, SizeToContent does both directions. Every
         hand-picked width tried here was wrong within a change or two, because what this window has to
         show kept growing: a flag, a country, a unit in a heading. Worse, a fixed width was being
         overruled anyway - the saved placement restored the width from the session before, so nothing
         set here ever reached the operator (WindowBounds now leaves a content-sized width alone).

         Measured, the columns want about 560 with ordinary callsigns and the two lines of text want
         about 400, so the TABLE is what sets the width and the sentences fit inside it comfortably.
         Letting the window ask the table means it is never wider than it needs to be - and it gets
         narrower by itself when the list holds only short callsigns.

         MaxHeight stops a long list from growing a window taller than the screen: past that point the
         table scrolls inside instead. It is a plain number and not a measurement of the monitor on
         purpose - the only API that answers that question (SystemParameters.WorkArea) speaks for the
         primary screen alone, and this program has been bitten by trusting it.

         LIGHT GREEN, and fixed - it does not follow the colour scheme. That is deliberate and it is
         why every colour in this window is spelled out below: the theme's TextBrush is a LIGHT colour
         in the dark schemes, and light text on a light green window cannot be read at all. A window
         that commits to its own colour has to bring its own text colour with it. -->
    <Window.Resources>
        <!-- Dark text everywhere in this window, whatever the rest of the program is wearing - and WHITE
             on the one row the right-click menu is about, which is painted dark green. An explicit
             Foreground on a TextBlock beats anything inherited from the row, so the exception has to be
             made here as well as on the cell, or the marked row would be dark text on a dark ground.
             The two sentences at the top of the window have no row behind them, so IsMarked never
             resolves for them and the trigger never fires. -->
        <Style TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#1A1A1A"/>
            <Style.Triggers>
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding IsMarked}" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FFFFFF"/>
                </DataTrigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>
        <!-- Background Transparent and NO IsSelected trigger: the grid's own selection must never paint
             anything here, because a left click selects and a left click has to leave the list looking
             as it found it. The row's own colour shows through instead - see RowStyle below. -->
        <Style TargetType="DataGridCell">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#1A1A1A"/>
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
            <Style.Triggers>
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding IsMarked}" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FFFFFF"/>
                </DataTrigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>
        <!-- EVERY ROW COLOUR IN ONE PLACE, striping included, and that is not tidiness - it is the only
             way the mark can win.
             DataGrid.RowBackground and AlternatingRowBackground are applied by the grid as a LOCAL VALUE
             on each row, and a local value outranks a style trigger however the trigger is written. With
             those two properties set on the grid, the mark's trigger below fired - the text really did
             turn white - and the row stayed white underneath it, so the marked row read as a row that
             had gone blank. Striping done here is a Setter and a trigger, which the mark can outrank.
             The mark's trigger is LAST because, among triggers setting the same property, the last
             active one wins. -->
        <Style x:Key="MarkableRow" TargetType="DataGridRow">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="#FFFFFF"/>
            <Style.Triggers>
                <Trigger Property="AlternationIndex" Value="1">
                    <Setter Property="Background" Value="#F1F8F2"/>
                </Trigger>
                <!-- The one row the right-click menu is about. Nothing else paints a row. -->
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding IsMarked}" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="Background" Value="#1B5E20"/>
                </DataTrigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>
        <!-- Close, in this window's own colours. The app-wide button follows the colour scheme, and
             its dark-mode face on a light green window would be a dark slab with dark text on it the
             moment the pointer arrived. -->
        <Style x:Key="GreenCloseButton" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="#FFFFFF"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#1A1A1A"/>
            <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="bd" Background="{TemplateBinding Background}" BorderBrush="#7CB47F"
                                BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="4" SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="#A8D8FF"/>
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="#3A80C8"/>
                            </Trigger>
                            <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="#7DC0F5"/>
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>
        <!-- The headers a shade deeper than the window, so the strip reads as a header without a
             border being drawn round it. -->
        <Style TargetType="DataGridColumnHeader">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="#C8E6C9"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#1A1A1A"/>
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/>
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="16"/>
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,5"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="#A5D6A7"/>
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,0,1,1"/>
        </Style>
        <!-- A heading centred over its column. The headings are left-aligned by default, which is right
             for a column of words but wrong over a column holding one centred button. BasedOn the
             implicit style above so it keeps the green face and the rest - and it has to be declared
             AFTER that style, because StaticResource can only see what has already been declared. -->
        <Style x:Key="CenteredHeader" TargetType="DataGridColumnHeader"
               BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type DataGridColumnHeader}}">
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        </Style>
    </Window.Resources>
    <!-- 6px above the text rather than 14. The band holding those two lines looked far taller than two
         lines of writing needs, and the margin was most of what was making it so - the text itself is
         two 16px lines, about 43px, and that part cannot shrink without going under the 16 this program
         writes everything at. Sides and bottom stay at 12 so the table is not jammed against the frame. -->
    <Grid Margin="12,6,12,12">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <!-- MinHeight so the "nobody on the list yet" line still has somewhere to be printed when
                 the last station is deleted or logged while this window is open. -->
            <RowDefinition Height="*" MinHeight="80"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- WHAT THE BUTTONS DO, and nothing else. This line used to open with "Stations you sent over
             from the cluster", which was the one sentence here that said something the window already
             says for itself - it is called Try Again and it is filled from a menu item called "Copy
             into Try Again". What is left is the part that cannot be worked out by looking: that the
             button drives the radio, and that a station takes himself off this list once he is worked. -->
        <!-- A LINE BREAK BETWEEN THE TWO SENTENCES, not left to the wrap. Wrapping broke the second
             sentence wherever the window's width happened to fall - "...once you" and then "log him."
             on the next line - so the two statements ran into one another and neither could be taken in
             at a glance. One sentence, one line. TextWrapping stays on as the safety net for a window
             dragged narrower than the text. -->
        <!-- "Try Again" in bold because it is the NAME OF THE BUTTON, not part of the sentence: the eye
             should be able to jump from these words to the green key on the row without reading the
             line. Plain Runs with literal text - no bindings here, so none of the two-way trouble that
             the Ago column's Runs ran into. -->
        <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" FontSize="16" Margin="2,0,2,6" TextWrapping="Wrap"><Run
                   Text="Press "/><Run Text="Try Again" FontWeight="Bold"/><Run
                   Text=" to put the radio on one."/><LineBreak/><Run
                   Text="A station leaves this list by itself once you log him."/></TextBlock>

        <!-- EVERY COLUMN THAT HOLDS TEXT IS AUTO WIDTH, so nothing is ever cut off - a truncated
             callsign or a frequency missing its last digit is worse than useless here. Auto measures
             the header as well as the cells, so a short column still fits its own title. Only the last
             column, the one holding the buttons, is "*": see the note on it.

             Sorting is fixed and the headers do not re-sort: newest at the top, always. The order is
             the answer to "who did I just see", and a column the operator sorted by accident would
             hide the row he had come to press. -->
        <DataGrid x:Name="Grid_TryAgain" Grid.Row="1"
                  AutoGenerateColumns="False" IsReadOnly="True" CanUserAddRows="False"
                  CanUserDeleteRows="False" CanUserSortColumns="False" CanUserReorderColumns="False"
                  HeadersVisibility="Column" SelectionMode="Single" SelectionUnit="FullRow"
                  GridLinesVisibility="Horizontal" RowHeaderWidth="0" FontSize="16"
                  Background="#FFFFFF" BorderBrush="#A5D6A7" BorderThickness="1"
                  HorizontalGridLinesBrush="#D7E9D8"
                  AlternationCount="2"
                  RowStyle="{StaticResource MarkableRow}"
                  PreviewMouseRightButtonDown="Grid_TryAgain_MouseRightButtonDown"
                  PreviewMouseLeftButtonDown="Grid_TryAgain_MouseLeftButtonDown">
            <DataGrid.Columns>
                <!-- HOW LONG AGO IT WENT ON THE LIST, leftmost, and the list is ordered by it.
                     There WAS a "Time (UTC)" column here showing the clock time it was copied in. It
                     went because it made the operator do the arithmetic it was trying to save him:
                     what he wants to know is how long the station has been waiting, and reading "22:14"
                     and subtracting it from the wall clock is how he had to find that out. This column
                     answers the question directly and counts up on its own while the window is open -
                     see the timer in the code. -->
                <DataGridTemplateColumn Header="Ago" Width="Auto" SortMemberPath="AddedUtc">
                    <DataGridTemplateColumn.CellTemplate>
                        <DataTemplate>
                            <!-- The NUMBER in bold, the unit beside it in plain text. "min" is the same
                                 three letters on every row; the figure is the only part that changes and
                                 the only part worth reading, so it is the part that carries the weight.

                                 Mode=OneWay is REQUIRED, not tidiness. Run.Text is registered to bind
                                 two-way by default - it has to be, so a RichTextBox can write back what
                                 the user types into it - and both of these properties are read-only,
                                 which WPF reports at run time as "a TwoWay or OneWayToSource binding
                                 cannot work on the read-only property". TextBlock.Text elsewhere in this
                                 file is one-way by default and needs nothing. -->
                            <TextBlock Padding="6,3" HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                       FontSize="16"><Run Text="{Binding AgoNumber, Mode=OneWay}" FontWeight="Bold"/><Run Text="{Binding AgoUnit, Mode=OneWay}"/></TextBlock>
                        </DataTemplate>
                    </DataGridTemplateColumn.CellTemplate>
                </DataGridTemplateColumn>
                <!-- THE FLAG SITS INSIDE THIS COLUMN, not in one of its own. It belongs to the callsign
                     - it is the same fact said in a picture - and a separate column would have cost a
                     header, a grid line and the width of both for nothing. The tooltip names the
                     country in words, since a flag alone is a guess for anyone who does not know it.

                     24x16 Uniform, the same size the cluster's flag column uses, so the two tables show
                     a country identically. A callsign the country file does not recognise simply has no
                     picture: the Image is left with nothing to show and takes its 24px quietly, which
                     keeps every callsign in the column starting at the same place. -->
                <DataGridTemplateColumn Header="Callsign" Width="Auto" SortMemberPath="DXCallsign">
                    <DataGridTemplateColumn.CellTemplate>
                        <DataTemplate>
                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                        Margin="6,3" ToolTip="{Binding Country}">
                                <Image Source="{Binding FlagPath}" Width="24" Height="16"
                                       Stretch="Uniform" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding DXCallsign}" FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="16"
                                           VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="6,0,0,0"/>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </DataTemplate>
                    </DataGridTemplateColumn.CellTemplate>
                </DataGridTemplateColumn>
                <!-- THE COUNTRY IN WORDS, beside the flag that is the same fact in a picture. Plain
                     text, no colour of its own - which is exactly how the cluster shows it: its Country
                     column is an ordinary text column, and only DX, Freq and Mode there carry meaning
                     in their colour.

                     AUTO like the rest. It was "*" while the window had a fixed width, to soak up the
                     leftover so no empty strip appeared on the right - but the window now takes its
                     width FROM these columns, so there is no leftover for anything to soak up, and a
                     star column under SizeToContent would only collapse to its minimum.

                     MaxWidth for the handful of enormous DXCC names - "Bonaire, Curacao (Neth
                     Antilles)" among them - one of which would otherwise stretch the whole window.
                     Trimming cuts it and the tooltip carries the full name, so nothing is really lost. -->
                <DataGridTextColumn Header="Country" Binding="{Binding Country}" Width="Auto" MaxWidth="150">
                    <DataGridTextColumn.ElementStyle>
                        <Style TargetType="TextBlock">
                            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,3"/>
                            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
                            <Setter Property="TextTrimming" Value="CharacterEllipsis"/>
                            <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{Binding Country}"/>
                        </Style>
                    </DataGridTextColumn.ElementStyle>
                </DataGridTextColumn>
                <!-- Kept exactly as the cluster spotted it, so Try Again tunes where the spot said.
                     Centred, not right-aligned: these are all cluster frequencies on the same few
                     bands, so they are the same length as each other and line up anyway - and centred
                     they sit under the middle of their own heading instead of away from it. -->
                <DataGridTemplateColumn Width="Auto" SortMemberPath="FreqText">
                    <!-- THE UNIT IN THE HEADING, where the cluster also puts it. These numbers come
                         straight off the cluster and are kHz - "7199.0" - while the same station in the
                         log below is written 7.199000 in MHz. Saying which is which is the difference
                         between reading the row and having to work it out. Bold and smaller: it is a
                         note on the heading, not a second heading.

                         STACKED, not side by side: "Frequency kHz" on one line measured 120px and was
                         the widest heading in the table, which made the unit cost 27px of window for
                         three characters. Underneath, it costs nothing at all - the column was already
                         that tall. The cluster writes its own frequency heading the same way. -->
                    <DataGridTemplateColumn.Header>
                        <TextBlock TextAlignment="Center" LineHeight="15"
                                   LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight">
                            <Run Text="Frequency"/><LineBreak/><Run Text="kHz" FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13"/>
                        </TextBlock>
                    </DataGridTemplateColumn.Header>
                    <DataGridTemplateColumn.CellTemplate>
                        <DataTemplate>
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding FreqText}" Foreground="{Binding FreqBrush}"
                                       FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="16" Padding="6,3"
                                       HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        </DataTemplate>
                    </DataGridTemplateColumn.CellTemplate>
                </DataGridTemplateColumn>
                <DataGridTextColumn Header="Mode" Binding="{Binding Mode}" Width="Auto">
                    <DataGridTextColumn.ElementStyle>
                        <Style TargetType="TextBlock">
                            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,3"/>
                            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
                            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center"/>
                        </Style>
                    </DataGridTextColumn.ElementStyle>
                </DataGridTextColumn>
                <!-- THE BUTTON, at the right end of the row, under the heading "Action". The heading was
                     blank at first, on the grounds that the button says what it does - but a nameless
                     column leaves a gap in the row of headings that reads as something missing, and
                     "Action" says what the column is for rather than repeating what the button says.
                     It is narrower than the button, so it costs no width.

                     AUTO, so the column is exactly as wide as the button and not a pixel more. It was
                     "*" for a while, to stop an empty strip appearing down the right-hand side of the
                     table - but that job has moved to the Country column, which can carry spare width
                     without looking odd. A button marooned in the middle of a wide empty lane looks
                     like a mistake. -->
                <DataGridTemplateColumn Header="Action" Width="Auto" CanUserResize="False"
                                        HeaderStyle="{StaticResource CenteredHeader}">
                    <DataGridTemplateColumn.CellTemplate>
                        <DataTemplate>
                            <!-- 30 high, not 28. The keycap gives its content the face height less 3px
                                 of key travel and 2px top and bottom, so a 28px key leaves 21 - and a
                                 bold 16px line needs a shade more than that. The letters that pay are
                                 the ones with a tail, which is both the y and the g in "Try Again".

                                 The 12px side margins are what make the COLUMN a few px wider without
                                 touching the button itself: the column is Auto, so it measures the cell
                                 content including this margin. Widening the button's own padding would
                                 have made the button bigger, which is not what was asked for. -->
                            <Button Content="Try Again" Style="{StaticResource GoActionButtonStyle}"
                                    FontSize="16" Height="30" Padding="12,0" Margin="12,3"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                    Click="Btn_Try_Click"
                                    ToolTip="Put the radio on this station and close this window"/>
                        </DataTemplate>
                    </DataGridTemplateColumn.CellTemplate>
                </DataGridTemplateColumn>
            </DataGrid.Columns>
        </DataGrid>

        <!-- Nothing to say when the list is empty except that it is. Sits over the grid rather than
             taking a row of its own, so it cannot change the layout when it appears. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="TB_Empty" Grid.Row="1" FontSize="16"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" Visibility="Collapsed"
                   TextAlignment="Center"
                   Text="Nobody on the list yet.&#x0a;Right-click a spot in the cluster and choose &quot;Copy into Try Again&quot;."/>

        <Button Grid.Row="2" x:Name="Btn_Close" Content="Close" FontSize="16" Width="110" Height="30"
                Style="{StaticResource GreenCloseButton}"
                HorizontalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,12,0,0" Click="Btn_Close_Click" IsCancel="True"/>
    </Grid>
</Window>
